  "image_name": "713184e7c44102a9198b899847638018.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how nitrous oxide (N2O) can be produced during nitrogen cycling through both biological and abiotic routes, with special emphasis on ammonia oxidation and denitrification. Overall, it shows that N2O is not generated by a single pathway, but can arise from several linked reactions involving NH4+, NH2OH, NO, NO2−, and NO3−.\n\nAt the top, the schematic compares three situations: purely biotic pathways, coupled biotic–abiotic pathways, and purely abiotic chemistry. In the biotic case, ammonia-derived routes in ammonia oxidizers and nitrate-derived routes in denitrifiers both lead to N2O through intermediates such as hydroxylamine, nitric oxide, and nitrite. The coupled section highlights interactions with Mn or Fe redox chemistry, suggesting that biologically produced intermediates can be further converted abiotically to N2O. The abiotic section emphasizes reactions among NO, NO2−, NH2OH, and metal oxidants/reductants.\n\nThe lower part places these pathways in cells. Ammonia-oxidizing bacteria, archaea, and comammox organisms use enzymes such as AMO and HAO, and may release intermediates that feed abiotic N2O formation or denitrification. Denitrifiers reduce nitrate stepwise toward N2, but incomplete reduction can release N2O. The figure’s main message is that microbial metabolism and surrounding chemical conditions jointly control N2O production.",
